\section{Literature review}
\label{sec:related}

Accurate kinematic and dynamic modeling of marine vessels has been 
a central concern in naval architecture and marine control systems 
for several decades. The Nomoto model, introduced in its first- and 
second-order forms, remains one of the most widely adopted 
representations for describing the yaw dynamics of surface ships 
under helm input~\cite{nomoto}. More comprehensive 
representations, including the three-degree-of-freedom maneuvering 
models formulated by Fossen, account for coupled surge, sway, and 
yaw dynamics and have become standard references in marine control 
literature~\cite{fossen}. Bicycle kinematic and unicycle 
models, originally developed for wheeled mobile robots, have also 
found application in USV guidance owing to their computational 
simplicity and suitability for low-speed maneuvering 
scenarios~\cite{structural}, ~\cite{dubin}. Despite the availability of 
these well-established model families, determining which model best 
governs a specific deployed vessel remains a largely unresolved 
problem in practice.

Classical system identification approaches address this through 
dedicated sea trials and regression-based parameter fitting to 
hydrodynamic databases~\cite{yoon2003identification, alexandersson2022system}. More recent efforts have 
explored recursive least squares and extended Kalman filter 
formulations for online parameter estimation~\cite{voss2004nonlinear}, as well as 
grey-box methods that blend physical structure with data-driven 
parameter learning~\cite{chen2022grey, herrero2012two}. While effective 
within their respective settings, all of these approaches 
presuppose knowledge of the governing model structure and require 
either explicit control input measurements or dedicated onboard 
sensing hardware. This limits their applicability in scenarios 
where only pose observations of the vessel are available and the 
underlying model class itself is unknown. Furthermore, physics-based 
identification methods require extensive hydrodynamic testing and 
model-specific parameter fitting — involving captive model tests 
with towing tanks, rotating arms, and planar motion mechanisms — 
which ties identified parameters to a specific vessel's geometry 
and actuator configuration, fundamentally limiting generalizability 
across vessel types~\cite{chen2022grey}. Critically, the overwhelming 
majority of these approaches operate within a dynamic modeling 
paradigm, requiring force measurements, inertial quantities, or 
hydrodynamic coefficients that are not directly observable from 
pose trajectories alone. The present work departs from this 
paradigm entirely by adopting a kinematic perspective, where 
classification is performed exclusively from observable pose and 
velocity quantities derived from the trajectory, without requiring 
any dynamic state information.

The emergence of machine learning has opened alternative avenues 
for identifying dynamical systems directly from state trajectory 
data. Sparse identification of nonlinear dynamics (SINDy), 
originally proposed by Brunton et al., demonstrated that governing 
equations can be recovered from trajectory observations via sparse 
regression over candidate function 
libraries~\cite{brunton2016discovering}. While powerful for equation 
discovery, the basic SINDy framework is sensitive to noise and 
requires accurate derivative information from clean measurements, 
limiting its reliability under real-world conditions. Recent 
extensions such as iNeural-SINDy address these limitations by 
combining neural networks with integral-form constraints and 
automatic differentiation to enable robust equation discovery from 
noisy and scarce data~\cite{forootani2025robust}. However, despite 
these advances, such symbolic approaches aim to recover governing 
equations rather than discriminate between a discrete set of 
candidate model classes from observed trajectories alone — a 
fundamentally different problem that remains unaddressed.

Classification of dynamical systems as a supervised learning 
problem has received growing attention in robotics and autonomous 
systems. Prior work has shown that motion response signatures 
embedded in state trajectory data encode sufficient discriminative 
information to identify system behavior without dedicated sensing 
modalities~\cite{sebastian2019support, sermanet2018time}. 
Analogous ideas have been explored in fault detection and 
structural health monitoring, where convolutional and recurrent 
architectures have been applied to classify dynamical system 
behavior directly from time-series 
measurements~\cite{xu2025research, zhao2017convolutional}. These 
works collectively support the hypothesis that trajectory 
observations alone can discriminate among distinct model classes, 
provided the feature representation is carefully designed to 
capture intrinsic dynamic signatures rather than superficial path 
geometry.

In the maritime domain, Automatic Identification System (AIS) 
data has been widely used for vessel classification. It is 
important to note that AIS encompasses a rich multi-modal data 
stream — beyond kinematic fields such as position, speed over 
ground, course, and heading, it also includes static vessel 
attributes such as ship dimensions, draft, vessel type codes, 
and MMSI identifiers, as well as voyage-related information. 
This breadth of information has enabled classification 
frameworks that fuse trajectory-derived behavioral features with 
static geometric metadata to distinguish vessel categories, using 
classifiers ranging from logistic regression and naive Bayes to 
ensemble methods and support vector 
machines~\cite{sheng2018research, zhou2019ship, 
huang2023ship}. While these works confirm that kinematic 
signatures embedded in vessel movement data carry discriminative 
value, they differ fundamentally from the present work in two 
respects. First, they rely on aggregated statistical features 
extracted from long-horizon AIS records combined with static 
vessel metadata, rather than operating on short-horizon pose 
sequences derived purely from position and heading observations. 
Second, and more critically, their classification objective is 
vessel type or operational behavior — not the underlying kinematic 
motion model governing the vessel's dynamics. Identifying whether 
a vessel is a fishing boat or a cargo ship is an entirely 
different problem from identifying whether its motion is governed 
by a Nomoto, Unicycle, Three-DOF, or Bicycle Kinematic model. 
No prior work in the maritime domain has formulated the latter as 
a data-driven classification problem solvable from pose 
observations alone.

For sequential and time-series data, recurrent architectures such 
as Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks have been widely adopted 
owing to their capacity to model long-range temporal 
dependencies~\cite{lstm}. However, LSTMs suffer 
from vanishing gradients over long sequences and are inherently 
sequential in computation, which limits training efficiency. 
Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCNs), introduced by Bai et al., 
address these shortcomings through dilated causal convolutions 
with residual connections, achieving large receptive fields while 
remaining fully parallelizable~\cite{bai2018empirical}. TCNs have 
demonstrated competitive or superior performance relative to 
recurrent architectures across sequence modeling benchmarks and 
have been applied to trajectory 
prediction~\cite{nikhil2018convolutional} and motion 
classification tasks~\cite{lea2017temporal}. Extensions combining 
TCN with Transformer attention mechanisms have further demonstrated 
advantages over recurrent architectures for early and accurate 
time-series classification~\cite{chen2022early}, reinforcing the suitability of convolutional temporal encoders for 
sequential classification tasks. Their ability to capture 
multi-scale temporal structure through exponentially increasing 
dilation factors makes TCNs particularly well-suited for encoding 
the dynamic signatures of vessel maneuvers across different time 
horizons.

Beyond supervised classification, contrastive learning has emerged 
as a powerful paradigm for learning structured embedding spaces 
that generalize across distributional variations. Self-supervised 
frameworks such as SimCLR and MoCo learn representations by 
contrasting augmented views of the same 
sample~\cite{he2020momentum, chen2020simple}, while supervised 
contrastive learning, proposed by Khosla et al., extends this to 
the fully labeled setting by treating all same-class samples as 
positives, enforcing simultaneous intra-class compactness and 
inter-class separation~\cite{khosla2020supervised}. This approach 
produces more robust and transferable representations compared to 
cross-entropy training alone, particularly under distribution 
shift conditions. In the time-series domain, contrastive 
objectives have been applied to learn invariant representations 
across sensor modalities~\cite{eldele2021time}, demonstrating 
robustness to input variation that is directly relevant to the 
maneuver diversity encountered in vessel trajectory data.

Taken together, the reviewed literature reveals a clear and 
unaddressed gap at the intersection of marine motion modeling, 
trajectory-based classification, and deep sequence learning. 
Existing ship identification methods operate predominantly within 
a dynamic modeling paradigm, requiring force measurements, 
hydrodynamic coefficients, explicit control inputs, or dedicated 
onboard sensing — none of which are available when only pose 
observations of the vessel exist. AIS-based maritime 
classification methods rely on multi-modal data streams combining 
static vessel metadata with long-horizon behavioral aggregates, 
and address vessel type or operational behavior rather than 
kinematic model identity. Symbolic data-driven approaches such as 
SINDy target equation discovery rather than discrete model 
classification. General dynamical system classification methods 
in robotics have not been extended to the problem of 
discriminating among candidate kinematic models of marine vessels 
from short-horizon pose-only trajectory observations. 
Furthermore, while TCN architectures and supervised contrastive 
learning have individually demonstrated strong performance in 
sequence modeling and representation learning, their combination 
for kinematic motion model classification in the maritime domain 
has not been previously explored. This work directly addresses 
all of these gaps by proposing a unified framework that derives 
dynamics-oriented kinematic features from raw pose trajectories 
and trains a TCN encoder under a joint contrastive and 
cross-entropy objective to classify the ego ship's motion model 
without access to control inputs, onboard sensors, dynamic force 
measurements, or prior model structure assumptions.
